// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  LIBRARY — «Вдъхновение» (#2980)
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
//  GAP-09 multi-file schema: new domain file. Generator + datasource remain
//  in base.prisma.
//
//  ── SCHEMA ONLY, and that ordering was decided rather than inherited ──
//
//  P5's, P8's and P9's standing rule ships a NEW TABLE one release before the
//  code that writes it, so a rollback to the previous image never queries an
//  object that is gone. P8.6 WEAKENED that rule — for an additive nullable
//  column on an EXISTING table, which carries neither risk — and this is not
//  that case: `Book` and `BookChapter` are new tables, so the rule applies at
//  full strength. The two reads, the seed subcommand and the
//  `PERSISTABLE_PATHS` entry land in the following release.
//
//  ── WHY TENANTLESS: a GLOBAL CATALOGUE, not a social surface ──
//
//  A book is reference data. Every farm reads the same text, exactly as every
//  farm reads the same `Unit`, `AgriEvent` and `SoilSample` rows — so both
//  models carry a `GLOBAL_CATALOGUE_MODELS` entry in
//  `tests/guardrails/global-catalogue-models.test.ts`, which is the POSITIVE
//  counterpart to the subtractive tenant guardrails: without an entry, "no
//  tenantId" is indistinguishable from "forgot the tenantId".
//
//  That is a different argument from `channels.prisma`'s, which is
//  tenantless because a channel is PERSON-scoped — and the difference decides
//  the session variable below, so the two are not interchangeable.
//
//  ── THE POLICY ADMITS BOTH SESSION VARIABLES, and that is the point ──
//
//  #1664 cost a whole feature: `UserBlock`'s SELECT policy keyed on
//  `app.user_id` while the enforcement ran under `app.actor_user_id`, so the
//  arm matched nothing, every read returned zero rows, and NOTHING ERRORED.
//  An unset session variable yields NULL.
//
//  Every other tenantless table in this repo picks ONE of the two, because
//  the row BELONGS to somebody and the policy has to name them. A book
//  belongs to nobody: there is one population — "is this caller
//  authenticated" — and both runners answer it. `runInUserContext` sets
//  `app.user_id`; `runInTenantContext` sets `app.actor_user_id`. So the arm
//  admits either, and the trap is removed rather than documented.
//
//  This widens NOTHING, and the test asserts it: the admitted population is
//  the same people under both contexts, exactly as #1664's
//  `user_block_select_actor` admitted the same two people in the other
//  context. The disclosure boundary here is `publishedAt`, not the caller.
//
//  ── `publishedAt` IS ENFORCED IN THE POLICY *AND* IN THE READ ──
//
//  This is the one property the library actually has to get right, because
//  the owner's constraint is that **publishing waits for their review of the
//  text**. The seed writes an UNPUBLISHED book; the owner reads it; a
//  deliberate act publishes it. So there is a window — the whole point of the
//  flag — in which production holds rows that must reach nobody.
//
//  #2980 §3(d) names the failure directly: it "works in the seed and leaks
//  through a read nobody filtered". A `where` clause is one line somebody
//  deletes while refactoring a list query. So the policy carries it too, and
//  an `app_user` query CANNOT see an unpublished book however the application
//  is rewritten. Two layers, one property — P5.5b's arrangement, where the
//  guard is the source half and the migration is the database half.
//
//  ── NO `app_user` WRITE ARM AT ALL ──
//
//  `Channel`'s reasoning verbatim: these rows are seeded and administered,
//  and a caller able to insert one could publish a text under the platform's
//  own name. Writing is privileged; the READ is policy-governed.
//
//  ── SIGNED-IN, NOT PUBLIC ──
//
//  Owner ruling, and the mechanism matters: `isPublicPath` opens a prefix for
//  EVERY METHOD on it, and this repo's rule is that a public surface must
//  also authenticate itself in the handler. A library is not a legal duty
//  (unlike the Art 16 notice route), nothing about it wants to be anonymous,
//  and a signed-in read costs the phone nothing — it already holds a session.
//  A marketing-page library later is a `/f/`-style surface and its own
//  decision.
//
//  ── NO ENCRYPTED COLUMN, and no classification is owed ──
//
//  A published book is intended to be widely read, so encryption would buy
//  nothing and would make the text unsearchable (`ChannelPost.body`'s
//  argument). It owes no `DELIBERATELY_PLAINTEXT` entry either, which is a
//  statement about this schema rather than a general licence: that guard
//  fires on a field carrying a MANIFEST FIELD NAME, and since #1222 the
//  manifest is per `(model, field)`. None of these columns collides —
//  `summary` is deliberately not spelled `description`, and `sourceNote` /
//  `licenseNote` are deliberately not spelled `notes`. Renaming one of them
//  to a manifest name is what would owe the entry.
//
//  It also holds no person column, so `personal-data-registry-complete` has
//  nothing to classify — a book is not somebody's data.
//

/// What a book is FOR, as a CODE the client localises.
///
/// An enum rather than a free-text array, on `ReportReasonCode`'s reasoning
/// (#1553): an unvalidated string list becomes a de facto taxonomy nobody owns,
/// and the phone renders an unknown code as a missing string. The server
/// refusing an unknown value at write time is what keeps the two in step — and
/// here the DATABASE refuses it, so a seed cannot introduce one either.
///
/// PROVISIONAL, exactly as `ReportReasonCode` is. `STRATEGY` is the only member
/// the owner has named; the vocabulary grows as books are added, and adding a
/// value is a trivial migration. The list is a CONTENT taxonomy and not a
/// surface name, so it is deliberately NOT governed by `nav-vocabulary.md`.
enum BookUseCase {
  STRATEGY
  /// «Управление» — Franklin, *The Way to Wealth* (#2980, owner 2026-11-13).
  ///
  /// The second member, and the first evidence that the PROVISIONAL note above
  /// meant it: a second book arrived and the vocabulary grew by one migration,
  /// which is what an enum buys over free text.
  ///
  /// FOUR places carry this value and the one easiest to miss is not a schema
  /// file: `scripts/library/seed-library.ts` validates the SEED FILE with its
  /// own `z.enum`, and that schema is `.strict()` — so a book file naming an
  /// unlisted code is refused before the database is consulted at all, with a
  /// readable error rather than a 22P02. Adding the value here and not there
  /// produces a refusal that looks like a bad staged file.
  MANAGEMENT
  /// «Земеделие» — Bailey, *The Holy Earth* (#2980, owner 2026-11-13).
  ///
  /// The THIRD member, and the first one that is about the farm itself rather
  /// than about how to run one. agrent-ios shelves it as «Земеделие»,
  /// case-blind, so the phone needed no change for it.
  ///
  /// The owner was asked before this landed rather than after. The decision
  /// reached this session through the iOS session, and an enum value is
  /// effectively one-way — PostgreSQL has no `ALTER TYPE ... DROP VALUE` — so
  /// a relayed ruling was not enough to spend it on. The alternative offered
  /// was filing a farming book under MANAGEMENT, which costs nothing now and
  /// a data migration later.
  ///
  /// The four places above apply unchanged, and the zod one bit again: the
  /// staged Bailey file was refused by `BookFileSchema` before this landed,
  /// which surfaces as a Zod parse failure on a file whose sha256 had just
  /// been checked on both sides of the copy — i.e. as a corrupt file rather
  /// than a missing enum value.
  FARMING
}

/// One book in «Вдъхновение». A GLOBAL CATALOGUE row — see the header.
model Book {
  id String @id @default(cuid())

  /// The stable, URL-safe identifier the detail read is keyed on.
  ///
  /// UNIQUE, which is what makes the seed idempotent BY CONSTRUCTION rather
  /// than by a flag somebody forgets to set — `Channel.key`'s and
  /// `NoticeScore.reportId`'s precedent. A renamed TITLE therefore keeps the
  /// book's identity, and a phone's offline copy stays addressable.
  slug String @unique

  /// ONE title, in the language of the TEXT — never a `titleBg` / `titleEn`
  /// pair.
  ///
  /// `Channel` carries both locales because a channel NAME is reference data
  /// standing in for UI copy. A book title is not copy: it NAMES AN ARTEFACT.
  /// «Изкуството на войната» is the name of our own Bulgarian translation
  /// from the public-domain classical Chinese, and rendering it as "The Art of
  /// War" in an English UI would name a DIFFERENT artefact — in this exact
  /// case the Giles 1910 translation, which #2980 avoids deliberately because
  /// Giles died in 1958 and EU protection runs to the end of 2028.
  ///
  /// So `locale` says which language the text is in, and the title is read in
  /// that language whatever the UI is set to.
  title String

  /// The author, in the same language as `title`, for the same reason.
  author String

  /// The language of the TEXT — `bg` for every book the owner has named.
  ///
  /// Not an enum: `Locale` in this codebase is the UI's two-language set, and a
  /// library may one day hold a text in a language the interface does not
  /// speak. Reusing that enum would make such a book unrepresentable.
  locale String

  /// A short blurb, in the book's own language, so the directory is choosable.
  ///
  /// Spelled `summary` and not `description` ON PURPOSE: `description` is a
  /// field name the encryption manifest carries for other models, and a
  /// plaintext column wearing a manifest name owes a `DELIBERATELY_PLAINTEXT`
  /// entry. The clearer name costs nothing and avoids the classification.
  summary String?

  /// Where the text came from, and under what licence. Both are displayed on
  /// the detail read, because a public-domain claim a reader cannot check is
  /// not a claim.
  sourceNote  String
  licenseNote String

  /// Monotonic INTEGER, bumped whenever the text changes.
  ///
  /// An integer and NOT a timestamp (#2980 §3(a)): the phone compares versions
  /// to decide whether to re-download, and a timestamp invites a string
  /// comparison and a clock question. `updatedAt` sits beside it and is for
  /// DISPLAY — the two answer different questions and neither substitutes.
  version Int @default(1)

  /// What this book is for, as codes. See `BookUseCase`.
  useCases BookUseCase[]

  /// NULL until the owner has read the text and published it.
  ///
  /// A nullable TIMESTAMP rather than a `published` boolean, this repo's
  /// convention for every such state (`archivedAt`, `deletedAt`,
  /// `removedByModerationAt`) — and here the moment is itself the record of
  /// the owner's review having happened.
  ///
  /// IT IS A DISCLOSURE BOUNDARY, not a display flag: `book_select` refuses an
  /// unpublished row to every `app_user` query, and the read filters on it
  /// as well. See the header on why both.
  publishedAt DateTime?

  createdAt DateTime @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt

  chapters BookChapter[]

  /// The directory read: published books, newest first.
  @@index([publishedAt])
}

/// One chapter of one book.
model BookChapter {
  id String @id @default(cuid())

  bookId String

  /// 1-based, and the chapter's identity within the book — so a re-seed
  /// updates a chapter rather than appending a second copy of it.
  number Int

  /// The chapter title, in the book's `locale`.
  title String

  /// The chapter's title in the ORIGINAL language, where there is one — e.g.
  /// the classical Chinese 始計 beside «Планиране».
  ///
  /// Optional because it is a property of a TRANSLATION, and a book written in
  /// its own language has none. Displayed, never matched on.
  originalTitle String?

  /// The body, one string per paragraph.
  ///
  /// A scalar list rather than one blob with separators, because the client
  /// renders paragraph by paragraph and splitting display text on a delimiter
  /// is a decision that silently changes with the text. The `null` / `[]`
  /// distinction a Prisma scalar list cannot express (see `User.bottomTabs`) is
  /// not wanted here: a chapter with no paragraphs is broken either way, and
  /// the migration's CHECK refuses one.
  paragraphs String[]

  book Book @relation(fields: [bookId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  /// The identity — and the FK index Layer B requires, since `bookId` leads it.
  @@unique([bookId, number])
}
